Z||000001||Gokart||09-17-1999||08:23 PM||||Will check that out, but I wonder why there is a plastic wall there? Wouldn't that defeat the whole purpose of the scoop to begin with? What was the factory logic with this?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Cooter||09-17-1999||09:32 PM||||While getting my 85 4cyl ready for paint, I noticed the baffle in the air intake. It seems that it is in there for noise reduction because of the way the big plastic box is built, it looks like the tuned resonators on other cars. I just used a die grinder to cut the baffle out of the way. All I noticed is that when the window is down, you can hear the intake noise when you step on the gas.||||reg||14
Z||000003||batboy||09-17-1999||10:25 PM||||If you have relocted the battery like I have on my Batmobile, you can remove some of the insulation on the opposite side of the air vent (on the battery side). There is a rectanglar opening there like what is on the vent side that will allow more air to enter the engine compartment from the outer wheel well that will help cooling a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-17-1999||10:41 PM||||that baffle keeps the majority of rain out as well as stops a bit of noise.<P>the main reason fo the QP intake is to take in cold air. It doesn't ram anything w/o help. One concern with the OEM design is that it may actually fight air intake at highway speed. Ever blow across the end of a straw in a full soda...... same problem could be an issue with the side intake.||||reg||1
